{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1 - RAG Overview: Understanding the Architecture",
  "description": "Follow a PDF from ingestion to a streamed answer, then optionally try the same flow locally.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "nextTour": "2 - Document Ingestion: From PDFs to Vectors",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Welcome to RAG",
      "description": "## Welcome to the RAG tour\n\nFollow a PDF through ingestion, retrieval, and answer generation in this sample. You'll see how LangChain.js connects the pieces and how the API streams the answer back to the browser.\n\nYou only need the repository open in VS Code with CodeTour installed. You don't need npm dependencies, Ollama, a running app, or an Azure account to explore the code. At the end, you can optionally try the flow locally.\n\nThis is tour 1 of 6."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/tutorial/03-understanding-rag.md",
      "line": 5,
      "title": "What is RAG?",
      "description": "## What is RAG?\n\n**Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)** finds relevant passages in your documents and gives them to a language model before it answers.\n\nFor example, if you ask what personal information Contoso Real Estate collects, the app can retrieve passages from the sample privacy policy instead of relying on the model's general knowledge. The retrieved text gives the model evidence to work with, but you should still check that it supports the answer."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/tutorial/03-understanding-rag.md",
      "line": 15,
      "title": "RAG Architecture: The Three Pillars",
      "description": "## The three stages of RAG\n\n1. **Ingestion:** Split PDFs into shorter passages, turn those passages into vectors (embeddings), and store them for later search.\n2. **Retrieval:** Embed the user's question and search for passages with similar vectors. This can find related passages even when they use different words from the question.\n3. **Generation:** Give the question and retrieved passages to the language model, then stream its answer back to the browser."
    },
    {
      "file": "README.md",
      "line": 33,
      "title": "Architecture Overview",
      "description": "## How this sample is assembled\n\nThe Lit frontend sends chat requests to an Azure Functions backend. The backend uses LangChain.js to connect the embedding model, vector store, and language model.\n\n- **On Azure:** Azure OpenAI provides embeddings and chat; Cosmos DB stores vectors and chat history; Blob Storage stores the original PDFs.\n- **Locally:** Ollama provides embeddings and chat; FAISS stores vectors on disk. The sample PDFs are served from the repository."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/documents-post.ts",
      "line": 122,
      "title": "The Two Core Endpoints",
      "description": "## Two endpoints power the RAG pipeline\n\n- **`POST /api/documents`** runs when you add a PDF. It extracts and splits the text, embeds the chunks, and stores them for retrieval. It saves the original PDF to Blob Storage when that storage is configured.\n- **`POST /api/chats/stream`** runs when you send a chat message. It searches those indexed chunks and streams a response using the retrieved context.\n\nAzure Functions registers both endpoints with `app.http()`. The `route` specifies the path after the `/api` prefix, `methods` lists the allowed HTTP methods, and `handler` points to the function that processes the request.\n\nSeparating these steps means asking another question does not require processing the PDFs again."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/documents-post.ts",
      "line": 15,
      "title": "Document Ingestion Function",
      "description": "## From PDF to searchable chunks\n\n`postDocuments` reads the uploaded file from a multipart form. `PDFLoader` extracts the text, and the handler records the filename in `metadata.source` for later citations. The text splitter creates overlapping chunks before the Azure OpenAI/Cosmos DB or Ollama/FAISS path embeds and stores them."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/chats-post.ts",
      "line": 41,
      "title": "Chat Query Handler",
      "description": "## From question to answer\n\n`postChats` reads the incoming messages, resolves the session, and selects the models, vector store, and chat history for the configured environment. It then builds the chain, retrieves passages relevant to the last message, and streams an answer."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/chats-post.ts",
      "line": 21,
      "title": "The RAG System Prompt",
      "description": "## The RAG system prompt\n\nThe chain inserts retrieved passages at `{context}`. The prompt tells the model to use only those sources, say when they don't contain enough information, and cite the filenames it relied on. It also asks for three short follow-up questions.\n\nThese are instructions, not guarantees: the model can still make unsupported claims or omit a citation. Open the source PDF and verify that the cited text actually supports the answer."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/chats-post.ts",
      "line": 95,
      "title": "Building the RAG Chain",
      "description": "## Combining the documents and question\n\n`createStuffDocumentsChain` combines the chat model, the system and user prompt templates, and a document template. The document template formats each passage as `[filename]: content`. A `RunnableWithMessageHistory` wrapper associates the interaction with the session's message history."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/chats-post.ts",
      "line": 116,
      "title": "The Retrieval Step",
      "description": "## Vector search in action\n\n`retriever.invoke(question)` embeds the question and searches for similar document chunks. The retriever is configured to return up to three chunks (`store.asRetriever(3)`). The chain inserts the formatted chunks into `{context}` so that the model can use the text and cite each source filename."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/api/src/functions/chats-post.ts",
      "line": 150,
      "title": "Streaming the Response",
      "description": "## How the answer streams back\n\nThe handler calls `ragChainWithHistory.stream(...)` with the question and retrieved context. `createJsonStream` turns each non-empty piece of the answer into one line of newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON):\n\n```json\n{\"delta\":{\"content\":\"Hello\",\"role\":\"assistant\"},\"context\":{\"sessionId\":\"...\"}}\n```\n\nThis lets the browser display the answer as pieces arrive rather than waiting for the complete text."
    },
    {
      "title": "Tour Complete - Next Steps",
      "description": "## You've completed the RAG overview\n\nChoose what to do next:\n\n- **Keep exploring without running the app:** Start **2 - Document Ingestion: From PDFs to Vectors** to see how the app loads PDFs, splits text, and stores embeddings.\n- **Try the full flow locally:** Follow **Run the sample locally with Ollama** in `README.md`, then **Verify your first answer** to check an answer against `data/privacy-policy.pdf`. If the model does not provide a usable citation, open the PDF directly and treat any claim you can't verify as unverified."
    }
  ]
}
